  <!-- GLH is the SAME protonated glutamate as GLUP above, under the three-character name that
       survives a PDB round-trip. A four-character residue name has no entry in OpenMM's
       residues.xml, so its residue is read with NO bonds at all, and the library's own on-disk
       name restore reads a three-character field and rewrites "GLUP" back to "GLU", silently
       colliding the protonated residue with every deprotonated glutamate in the chain. "GLH" is
       already an OpenMM alternate name for GLU (pdbNames.xml), so the reader applies the full
       GLU bond set (which includes the OE2 to HE2 bond), and it is the name the library's own
       protonation_variant_name() produces for a protonated glutamate. Per-state proton transfer
       to a glutamate (EVB acid/base chemistry) needs this name to be templatable. -->
